 The area of a parallelogram is given by the formula \( \text{Area} = \text{side}_1 \times \text{side}_2 \times \sin(\theta) \), where \(\theta\) is the angle between the sides. Here, \(\text{side}_1 = s\), \(\text{side}_2 = 2s\), and \(\theta = 45^\circ\). The area is given as \(8\sqrt{2}\) square units. So we can write: \[
\text{Area} = s \cdot 2s \cdot \sin(45^\circ) = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Since \(\sin(45^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\), we get: \[
2s^2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Simplifying, we get: \[
s^2 \cdot \sqrt{2} = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Dividing both sides by \(\sqrt{2}\), we get: \[
s^2 = 8
\] Taking the square root of both sides, we get: \[
s = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}
\] So the value of \(s\) is \(2\sqrt{2}\). </think> <
